        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Paver Sealer",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-paver-sealer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which paver sealer is best for most patios?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a typical concrete paver patio where you want richer color, a quality acrylic like Foundation Armor AR350 is a strong default, assuming solvent products are legal where you live. If you prefer pavers to look untouched and want the least upkeep, a silane-siloxane penetrant such as Foundation Armor SX5000 WB is the better choice. Decide on the look first, then pick the chemistry that delivers it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does paver sealer make the surface slippery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Penetrating sealers do not change surface texture, so slip resistance stays about where it was. Film-forming acrylics, especially wet-look ones, reduce traction when wet. On pool decks, steps and sloped walks, either use a penetrant or mix an anti-slip grit additive into the final coat. Most sealer brands sell a compatible grit, and it is nearly invisible once applied."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should new pavers sit before sealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most paver makers suggest waiting 60 to 90 days so that efflorescence has a chance to surface and the joint sand can settle. If the pavers show a white bloom, clean it off with an efflorescence remover first. Some contractors seal sooner when the pavers look clean, but sealing over active efflorescence is a common cause of cloudy patches that are hard to fix."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will sealer stop weeds from growing in the joints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A film sealer flooded into dry joint sand binds the top layer of grains, which makes it harder for windblown seeds to root and for ants to dig. It reduces weeds but does not eliminate them. Polymeric sand does a better job on its own. Penetrating sealers do not bind joint sand at all, so pair them with polymeric sand if weeds are a problem."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use concrete driveway sealer on pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes. Many acrylic and penetrating products are labeled for both slabs and pavers. The difference is the joints: a paver sealer intended to stabilize sand is formulated to soak into and bind the joint material, and pavers often have a textured face that takes more product. Check that the label lists pavers specifically before you buy a product made mainly for poured concrete."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I fix a cloudy or white sealer finish?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With a solvent acrylic, the cloudiness can often be re-melted by rolling on a thin coat of the same sealer or a light pass of xylene on a dry, warm day. Water-based acrylics usually cannot be re-dissolved, so a hazy water-based coat typically has to be stripped and redone. Either way, find the cause, which is almost always moisture, before you recoat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it worth sealing pavers at all?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. Clay brick and dense pavers in a dry climate may do fine unsealed, and unsealed pavers never peel or need stripping. Sealing is worth it when you want color enhancement, when joint sand keeps washing out, when you fight oil and food stains, or when freeze-thaw cycles and road salt are wearing the surface. Match the product to the problem you actually have."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
