            These picks come from manufacturer data sheets, labeled coverage rates and solids content, chemistry type, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We did not run our own field trials; where a claim comes from the label, we say so.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="finish-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Choose the Finish Before You Choose the Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every paver sealer on the shelf falls into one of three looks, and deciding which one you want removes about two-thirds of the options. <strong>Natural</strong> leaves the pavers looking exactly as they do when dry. <strong>Enhanced or satin</strong> deepens the color a shade or two with little shine. <strong>Wet look</strong> makes the patio look like it just rained, with noticeable gloss. None of them is better in the abstract. The right answer depends on the paver, how the space is used, and how much upkeep you will tolerate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet-look finishes are the most dramatic and the most demanding. They come from acrylic films that sit on top of the paver, and that film is what produces the shine. It also wears in traffic lanes, can turn cloudy if moisture is trapped underneath, and gets slippery when wet. On a covered patio or a front walk that mostly gets admired, a wet look can be worth it. On a pool deck where kids run barefoot, it usually is not, unless you add grit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural finishes come from penetrating sealers that soak into the paver and line the pores with a water repellent. They do not change color, do not add slip, and do not peel because there is no film. They also do nothing to revive faded pavers. If your pavers still look good and you mainly want to slow staining, salt damage and freeze-thaw wear, a natural finish is the low-maintenance answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle ground, enhanced or satin, is where most homeowners end up after seeing a sample. These are thinner films or penetrating enhancers that bring back color without a mirror shine. Before you commit, seal one paver in an out-of-the-way spot, or a spare paver left over from the install, and look at it dry and wet. Ten minutes of testing beats living with a patio you dislike for three years.
          </p>

