        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Film-Forming vs Penetrating: What Is Actually on the Paver
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film-forming sealers are mostly acrylics, with a few polyurethane and hybrid products at the premium end. They leave a thin plastic layer, usually a few thousandths of an inch thick, bonded to the top of the paver. That layer blocks stains, holds a sheen and, when it is flooded into the joints, can glue the top of the joint sand together. The cost is maintenance: films wear, scuff and eventually need a fresh coat or a strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are silanes, siloxanes, siliconates or blends of them. Their molecules are small enough to travel into the capillaries of the paver, where they react and leave a water-repellent lining. Water beads on the surface instead of soaking in, and that matters in cold climates because water inside a paver expands roughly 9 percent when it freezes. Less water in the paver means less spalling and less salt damage over the years.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a hybrid category, often sold as penetrating enhancers, that soaks in like a silane but carries resins that deepen color. These are worth knowing about if you want a richer tone on a driveway, where a surface film would wear off under tires. They usually cost more per gallon and still give less shine than a true wet-look acrylic, but they hold their look longer in high-wear spots.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Film for looks and joint sand, penetrating for durability.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your priority is color and locking down loose sand, buy a film-forming acrylic and accept the upkeep. If your priority is protection with almost no maintenance, buy a silane-siloxane penetrant and pair it with polymeric sand. Trying to get both from one cheap product is how patios end up blotchy.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One practical rule follows from all this: you can usually put a penetrating sealer down first and a film later, but not the other way around. A penetrant cannot soak through an existing acrylic film. If your pavers already have an old acrylic coat, you either reseal with a compatible acrylic or strip the old film before switching to a penetrant. For a deeper look at the repellent chemistry, see our guide to <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane sealers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solvent-or-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Solvent-Based or Water-Based: The Trade-Offs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solvent-based acrylics carry their resin in xylene, acetone or similar solvents. They wet out the paver deeply, give the strongest color pop, and dry fast even on cool days. Their best feature for a DIY job is that the film can be re-dissolved. If a section clouds up or shows roller marks, a pass with more sealer or a little xylene usually re-melts it and lets it level out. That forgiveness is why many contractors still prefer them for wet-look work.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downsides are odor, flammability and regulation. Solvent sealers are restricted or reformulated in states with tight VOC limits, including California and much of the Northeast, so the product you see online may not ship to you. You need a respirator rated for organic vapors, no ignition sources nearby, and pets and kids kept away until it flashes off. Solvent acrylics can also soften some polymeric sands and older water-based coatings, so check compatibility first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers have improved a great deal. The better ones now match solvent products for stain resistance and come close on color enhancement, with mild odor and soap-and-water cleanup. They are the safer choice next to a house, a pool or a garden. Our separate roundup of <a href="/best-water-based-paver-sealer-reviews/">water-based paver sealers</a> goes deeper on that category alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch with water-based acrylics is the weather window. The resin particles need warmth and dry air to fuse into a clear film, and once cured they will not re-melt. Apply them on a damp morning or over pavers that still hold moisture and the coat can dry milky, which usually means stripping rather than touching up. Most labels want air and surface temperatures between about 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit and no rain for 24 hours.
          </p>

