        <div id="paver-material" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Match the Sealer to the Paver Material
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers are the most common and the least fussy. They accept every sealer type, from penetrants to wet-look acrylics. New concrete pavers often carry efflorescence, the white powdery bloom of salts migrating out of the cement, during their first months. Sealing over that bloom locks it under the film, so most manufacturers suggest waiting 60 to 90 days after installation, then cleaning with an efflorescence remover before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clay brick pavers are dense and fired, so they absorb far less sealer than concrete. A heavy film can sit on top, refuse to bond and peel. A penetrating sealer or a thin enhancer is usually the better match. Clay also tends to hold its color for decades because the color is fired through the brick rather than added as pigment, so the main reason to seal clay is stain and moss resistance, not color restoration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural stone is the category where the wrong product causes the most damage. Travertine, limestone and sandstone are soft and porous, and they need to breathe. Trapping moisture under an acrylic film can cause whitening or flaking. Slate and granite are denser and absorb little. For all of them, a breathable impregnating sealer made for stone is the safe default, and if you want a richer tone, use a stone-specific enhancer rather than a concrete wet-look product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Permeable pavers are a special case. Their whole purpose is to let water drain through wide gravel-filled joints. Do not flood a film sealer into those joints, because it can reduce how well the system drains. Seal the faces only, preferably with a penetrant, and keep the joint aggregate clean so the system keeps working as designed.
          </p>

