        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Prep Work That Decides Whether the Sealer Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by removing everything you do not want sealed in for the next few years. Oil, rust, tannin stains from leaves, and moss all look worse under a sealer than they did before. Spot-treat oil with a degreaser or poultice, use an oxalic-acid rust remover on fertilizer and furniture stains, and kill moss and algae before washing. Our guide to the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> covers the grease problem in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, wash the pavers with a paver cleaner and a pressure washer, ideally with a surface cleaner attachment to avoid zebra stripes. Keep the pressure moderate, around 1,500 to 2,500 psi with a wide fan tip, and keep the nozzle moving. Blasting at close range etches the paver face and digs out joint sand. If efflorescence is present, treat it with an efflorescence cleaner after washing and rinse thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then deal with the joints. Washing almost always pulls sand out, and a sealer cannot stabilize sand that is not there. Refill joints to about an eighth of an inch below the bevel, using regular joint sand if you plan to stabilize with a film sealer, or polymeric sand if you are using a penetrant. Our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> covers the activation and cure times you need to respect before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, wait for the pavers to dry. This is the step people skip, and it is behind most cloudy finishes. After a thorough wash, give concrete pavers at least 24 hours of dry weather, and 48 hours if the weather is cool or humid. A simple test is to tape a square of plastic sheet to a paver overnight. If the underside fogs with moisture by morning, the pavers are not ready.
          </p>

