        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Apply Paver Sealer Without Haze or Lap Marks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use the tool the label calls for. Most film sealers go on best with a pump sprayer followed by back-rolling with a medium-nap (3/8 inch) roller, which pushes product into the paver texture and spreads puddles before they dry. Penetrating sealers are usually applied by low-pressure sprayer until the surface is saturated but not puddled, then any standing liquid is spread out with a roller or broom. Use a sprayer with seals rated for solvents if you are using a solvent product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in small sections and keep a wet edge. Pick a starting point away from the exit, spray a section a few feet wide, roll it, and move on while the edge of the last section is still wet. Overlapping onto a half-dry edge is what causes lap marks. Do not work in direct midday sun on a hot day. The surface can be 30 degrees warmer than the air, and the sealer will skin over before it levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thin coats are the rule that matters most. Two thin coats almost always look better and last longer than one heavy coat, because a thick film traps solvent or water and is more likely to blush white or peel. If the label gives a coverage rate, measure the patio and portion the sealer so you are not dumping product. On the joints, flood them on the first coat if you are stabilizing sand, then keep the second coat to the faces.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the space gets wet often, add slip resistance. Many film sealer makers sell a fine polymer grit that you stir into the final coat or broadcast onto it while wet. It is nearly invisible and makes a real difference on pool decks and steps. Keep foot traffic off for the time on the label, usually a few hours for light walking and 24 to 72 hours for furniture, and keep cars off for longer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE YOU OPEN THE PAIL</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the next 24 hours of weather, not just today.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rain, heavy dew or a sudden temperature drop within the cure window can leave a film sealer cloudy or soft. If rain is forecast within a day, wait. A delayed weekend costs nothing; a stripped patio costs a full weekend plus the price of stripper.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Coverage, Quantity and What It Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage varies with the paver more than the product. Smooth, dense pavers might take 150 to 200 square feet per gallon on the first coat. Rough, porous or tumbled pavers can drink a gallon every 80 to 120 square feet, and flooding joint sand adds more on top. Plan for the low end of the label range on the first coat and a higher spread rate on the second, since the pavers will be partly sealed by then.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the math before you buy. A 400-square-foot patio with tumbled pavers, two coats, and flooded joints could easily need 6 to 8 gallons of a film sealer. The same patio with a penetrating silane might need 3 to 4 gallons for a single saturating coat. Buying in 5-gallon pails is almost always cheaper per gallon and keeps the batch consistent, which matters for color-enhancing products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price per gallon tells only part of the story. At the time of writing, mainstream acrylic paver sealers run roughly $30 to $60 a gallon, premium penetrants and stone impregnators roughly $50 to $120 a gallon, and contractor-sized pails bring the per-gallon price down. A penetrating sealer that lasts six or more years per its maker can cost less per year than a cheap acrylic you redo every two years, even if the pail costs twice as much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring out the job typically costs about $1.50 to $3 per square foot for cleaning, re-sanding and sealing, more in high-cost metros or for natural stone. The labor is mostly in the cleaning and joint work, which is exactly the part that decides the result. If you DIY, put the time there and spend less worrying about which brand of sealer to buy.
          </p>

