        <div id="resealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When and How to Reseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film sealers tell you when they are done. The sheen fades in traffic lanes first, water stops beading and starts darkening the paver, and joint sand begins washing out again. On most patios that happens in two to four years. Penetrating sealers fade more quietly. Splash water on the surface every spring; when it soaks in rather than beading on more than a few pavers, it is time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resealing with the same chemistry is the easy path. Clean the pavers, let them dry, and apply a single thin maintenance coat of the same type. Stacking coat after coat of film sealer for a decade, though, builds a thick layer that is prone to whitening and peeling. If the existing film is already cloudy, flaking or yellowed, strip it before recoating rather than burying the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switching chemistry takes more care. Water-based acrylic over an intact solvent acrylic often fails to bond; solvent over water-based can wrinkle or lift the old coat. A penetrant over any film cannot reach the paver. The safe sequence is to test a small area, wait a few days and check adhesion with a strip of masking tape. If the old coat lifts with the tape, you are stripping before resealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not ignore what is under the sealer, either. Settled pavers, poor drainage and edges without restraint will ruin any finish. If you are pairing a new sealer with re-sanded joints, our roundup of the <a href="/best-polymeric-sand-for-pavers/">best polymeric sand for pavers</a> covers which sands tolerate a sealer on top. For poured concrete next to the pavers, the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealer</a> guide is the better starting point, since slabs and pavers do not always want the same product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Troubleshooting a Sealed Patio
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>White or milky patches.</strong> Haze almost always means moisture got trapped, either because the pavers were damp, dew settled before the coat cured, or the film went on too thick. On solvent acrylics, wait for a warm, dry afternoon and roll a light coat of xylene or fresh sealer over the area to let the film re-flow and release the moisture. On water-based films, a light scrub sometimes helps, but full removal with a stripper is the dependable fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Peeling or flaking.</strong> A film that lifts in sheets usually went over dirt, efflorescence, an incompatible older coating, or a dense paver it could not grip. Patching rarely blends in. Strip the affected area, clean it properly, and reseal the whole field so the sheen matches. If peeling keeps returning on clay brick or dense stone, switch to a penetrating sealer that does not depend on surface adhesion.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dark, blotchy color.</strong> Uneven darkening comes from uneven absorption: puddles that were never rolled out, joints flooded right next to dry faces, or pavers that were wetter in some spots than others when sealed. Light blotching often evens out after a few weeks of sun. Heavy blotching under a solvent film can sometimes be leveled by re-melting; under a water-based film it usually means stripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Joint sand still washing out.</strong> If sand keeps disappearing after a joint-stabilizing sealer, the joints were probably too low or too damp when you flooded them, or water is running across the patio in a concentrated stream from a downspout. Redirect the water first, then refill the joints and re-flood them on a dry day. For chronic problems, polymeric sand is the more permanent repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sealer tracked onto the house.</strong> Overspray on siding, doors and nearby stone is far easier to deal with fresh than cured. Mask thresholds, garage door bottoms and adjacent masonry before spraying, keep a rag and the right solvent on hand, and spray on a calm day. A light breeze can carry solvent sealer mist onto cars parked surprisingly far away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing pavers that are still damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture trapped under a film sealer is the leading cause of the milky white haze that shows up a day or two later. After washing, wait at least a full day of dry weather, longer in cool or humid conditions. The plastic-sheet test is cheap insurance before you open a five-gallon pail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying it on thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat feels like better protection, but thick films trap solvent or water, blush white, and peel sooner. Two thin coats at the label coverage rate look better and last longer. If product is puddling in low spots or pooling along edges, roll it out before it starts to set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                That white chalky bloom on new concrete pavers is mineral salt working its way out. Seal over it and it stays locked under the film, sometimes for years. Wait out the first season if you can, clean the bloom with an efflorescence remover, rinse thoroughly, and only then seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a wet-look film on a pool deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glossy acrylic films get slick when wet, and pool decks are wet constantly. Either choose a penetrating sealer that leaves the surface texture unchanged, or add an anti-slip grit to the final coat. Barefoot traffic on smooth wet film is the kind of risk that is easy to avoid.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing chemistries without a test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting water-based sealer over an old solvent coat, or the reverse, can produce wrinkling, poor adhesion or haze. Before resealing an old patio with a different product, test a small hidden area, let it cure several days, and check adhesion with masking tape before doing the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which paver sealer is best for most patios?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical concrete paver patio where you want richer color, a quality acrylic like Foundation Armor AR350 is a strong default, assuming solvent products are legal where you live. If you prefer pavers to look untouched and want the least upkeep, a silane-siloxane penetrant such as Foundation Armor SX5000 WB is the better choice. Decide on the look first, then pick the chemistry that delivers it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does paver sealer make the surface slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating sealers do not change surface texture, so slip resistance stays about where it was. Film-forming acrylics, especially wet-look ones, reduce traction when wet. On pool decks, steps and sloped walks, either use a penetrant or mix an anti-slip grit additive into the final coat. Most sealer brands sell a compatible grit, and it is nearly invisible once applied.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should new pavers sit before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most paver makers suggest waiting 60 to 90 days so that efflorescence has a chance to surface and the joint sand can settle. If the pavers show a white bloom, clean it off with an efflorescence remover first. Some contractors seal sooner when the pavers look clean, but sealing over active efflorescence is a common cause of cloudy patches that are hard to fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealer stop weeds from growing in the joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A film sealer flooded into dry joint sand binds the top layer of grains, which makes it harder for windblown seeds to root and for ants to dig. It reduces weeds but does not eliminate them. Polymeric sand does a better job on its own. Penetrating sealers do not bind joint sand at all, so pair them with polymeric sand if weeds are a problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use concrete driveway sealer on pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. Many acrylic and penetrating products are labeled for both slabs and pavers. The difference is the joints: a paver sealer intended to stabilize sand is formulated to soak into and bind the joint material, and pavers often have a textured face that takes more product. Check that the label lists pavers specifically before you buy a product made mainly for poured concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fix a cloudy or white sealer finish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a solvent acrylic, the cloudiness can often be re-melted by rolling on a thin coat of the same sealer or a light pass of xylene on a dry, warm day. Water-based acrylics usually cannot be re-dissolved, so a hazy water-based coat typically has to be stripped and redone. Either way, find the cause, which is almost always moisture, before you recoat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth sealing pavers at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Clay brick and dense pavers in a dry climate may do fine unsealed, and unsealed pavers never peel or need stripping. Sealing is worth it when you want color enhancement, when joint sand keeps washing out, when you fight oil and food stains, or when freeze-thaw cycles and road salt are wearing the surface. Match the product to the problem you actually have.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

